End-to-end CLI check (the same program run as a binary):

```
$ echo '{"sqes":[{"user_data":1,"op":"read","flags":["IO_LINK"],"res":-5},
                  {"user_data":2,"op":"write","res":0},
                  {"user_data":3,"op":"nop"}],
        "sq_array":[0,1,2],
        "events":[{"user_data":1,"res":-5}]}' | go run .

{
  "cqes": [
    { "user_data": 1, "res": -5 },
    { "user_data": 2, "res": -125 }
  ]
}
```

Here SQE 1 (`-5`) severs its link, SQE 2 settles as `-ECANCELED` (`-125`), and SQE 3 is an independent `nop` that simply has no completion in the trace — demonstrating that cancellation follows the *issue-order* chain rather than array adjacency.

